{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Arthur awoke to the sound of birds chirping, a sound he'd always considered mundane. But today, the birdsong felt…programmed. He sat up in bed, the floral wallpaper a slightly too-perfect pattern, and noticed the way the sunlight streamed through the window – pixelated, almost. He reached for his glasses, and the lenses seemed to shimmer with a subtle, digital glow. A wave of nausea washed over him, not from illness, but from an overwhelming sense of…wrongness. He glanced at his reflection in the mirror. His face was…crisp, unnaturally smooth, and his eyes held a vacant, almost too-wide expression. It wasn't *him*. He tried to speak, to call out, but the words felt forced, like a pre-written dialogue. A notification popped up in his vision: \"Level Up! Experience Points gained: 100.\" Arthur stared, dumbfounded, at the text. This wasn’t his bedroom. This wasn't his life.\n\n***\n\nPanic surged. He stumbled out of bed, his movements jerky and unnatural, as if controlled by an unseen force. The hallway was spotless, the walls painted in a vibrant, almost offensively cheerful shade of blue. NPCs – non-player characters – strolled past, going about their daily routines with robotic precision. A woman watering flowers in a perfectly manicured garden gave him a polite, yet unnerving, nod. \"Good morning, Player Arthur,\" she chirped. \"Enjoy your day!\" Arthur wanted to scream, to break free from this digital prison, but his limbs felt heavy, unresponsive. He was a character in a game, a pawn in someone else's elaborate creation. The realization crashed over him with the force of a system error, a terrifying and inescapable truth: his reality had been overwritten, and he was trapped inside.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Arthur", "logprob": -0.7662928104400635}, {"token": "▁awoke", "logprob": -0.5493413209915161}, {"token": "▁to", "logprob": -0.0029903003014